# core.air
#
# The documentation catalogue for the Air builtin library. Each form declares
# the documentation for one name, or for a family of names that share a
# description:
#
#     (docdef :category name…  "docstring")
#
# The category is an optional keyword; every name before the first string is a
# raw symbol (or a form of them); the remaining elements are strings, joined
# with newlines and deindented like a `def` docstring. The first line of a
# docstring is conventionally the call's spelling, and the rest is prose.
#
# The file is read when AirLang loads and installed in `BUILTIN_DOCS`
# (src/builtins_docs.jl). `(doc NAME)` shows one entry, `(doc .air)` shows the
# whole index, and `(help)` prints an introduction to the language. Names are
# checked against the registry by test/doc.jl, so every builtin, special form,
# and operator must appear here.
#
# This file is data, not a program: `docdef` registers documentation and
# returns `naught`, so loading it is harmless but unnecessary.

# ---- core: forms, values, and access -----------------------------------------

(docdef :core form?
  "(form? x)
   Whether x is a form at all -- always true, since every air value is a form.
   Provided so that user code can be polymorphic-looking and explicit.")

(docdef :core (coll? seq? map? associative? counted?)
  "(coll? x), (seq? x), (map? x), (associative? x), (counted? x)
   Shape predicates. A collection is a form with elements; a sequence is a
   collection whose elements are ordered; a map is a collection with map
   entries; an associative collection can be indexed by key; a counted
   collection reports a length. A scalar is not a collection, and naught is
   the empty collection.")

(docdef :core (atom? imm? mut?)
  "(atom? x), (imm? x), (mut? x)
   Whether x is a scalar (atom), an immutable form, or a mutable (a `mut`
   volatile). Almost everything in air is immutable; a mutable is the one
   value that changes in place.")

(docdef :core (empty? naught? present? blank?)
  "(empty? x), (naught? x), (present? x), (blank? x)
   Emptiness tests. naught is the empty form, `()`; empty? also holds of an
   empty string or an empty container; present? is its negation over naught
   and the empty string; blank? is present? over the empty string too.")

(docdef :core (bool? true? false?)
  "(bool? x), (true? x), (false? x)
   Whether x is a Boolean, and whether it is true or false. Only true is
   truthy in a condition; every other value -- including 0, an empty string,
   and :false -- is falsy.")

(docdef :core (int? integer? float? complex? number?)
  "(int? x), (integer? x), (float? x), (complex? x), (number? x)
   Numeric-type predicates. Integers are Int64 or BigInt; integer? accepts the
   rationals and floats whose value is integral; float? is Float64 or
   BigFloat; complex? is ComplexF64 or Complex{BigFloat}; number? accepts any
   leaf of the numeric tower.")

(docdef :core (string? symbol? keyword? wildcard?)
  "(string? x), (symbol? x), (keyword? x), (wildcard? x)
   Scalar-kind predicates: a string, a symbol (f), a keyword (:f), or a
   wildcard (?f, ?&rest, ?*kwargs, ??).")

(docdef :core (zero? even? odd? positive? negative? nan? infinite?)
  "(zero? x), (even? x), (odd? x), (positive? x), (negative? x), (nan? x),
   (infinite? x)
   Value predicates; each is false on a non-number rather than an error, so
   they compose with pattern guards. The elementwise tests that a tensor needs
   are the separate verbs isnan, isinf, iseven, and isodd.")

(docdef :core (same? eq?)
  "(same? a b), (eq? a b)
   Identity and equivalence. same? is identity; eq? is equivalence: scalars
   match by value and type (so (eq? 1 1.0) is false), map order is irrelevant,
   and a mutable matches only itself. Content equality is the == operator,
   which is numeric-friendly ((== 1 1.0) is true) and, like Julia, false for
   NaN against itself. hash follows eq?.")

(docdef :core (contains? member?)
  "(contains? coll x), (member? coll x)
   Whether x is an element of the collection (or, for a map, a value), under
   eq?.")

(docdef :core all?
  "(all? pred coll)
   True when (pred x) is truthy for every element of coll, and for an empty
   collection.")

(docdef :core any?
  "(any? pred coll)
   True when (pred x) is truthy for at least one element of coll.")

(docdef :core none?
  "(none? pred coll)
   True when (pred x) is falsy for every element of coll.")

(docdef :core (starts-with? ends-with?)
  "(starts-with? s prefix), (ends-with? s suffix)
   String-prefix and string-suffix tests; false on a non-string.")

(docdef :core inc
  "(inc x)
   x plus one; stays symbolic on a non-number.")

(docdef :core dec
  "(dec x)
   x minus one; stays symbolic on a non-number.")

(docdef :core value
  "(value x)
   Dereference a mutable (or force a delay or read a promise); for any other
   form, the form itself, since every value is a form.")

(docdef :core seqpart
  "(seqpart x)
   The sequence part of a form, with its map entries dropped.")

(docdef :core mappart
  "(mappart x)
   The map part of a form, with its sequence elements dropped.")

(docdef :core push
  "(push coll x…)
   A copy of coll with x… appended; a table appends rows from positional
   mappings and columns from map entries. Pure: the argument is unchanged.")

(docdef :core pop
  "(pop coll)
   A copy of coll without its last sequence element (or, for a table, its
   last row).")

(docdef :core drop
  "(drop coll key)
   A copy of coll without the entry at key; a lone : drops every entry of the
   level. For a table, drop removes a row, a column, or sets a cell to NA.")

(docdef :core length
  "(length x)
   The number of entries in a form: sequence elements plus map entries. A
   table reports its row count, a tensor its element count, and a scalar 1.")

(docdef :core haskey
  "(haskey coll key)
   Whether the collection has an entry at key.")

(docdef :core get
  "(get coll key…; hold=false)
   The single accessor. An integer key indexes a sequence (1-based; a
   negative key counts from the end; 0 is the whole form; n + 1 appends in a
   setter); a keyword -- or a bare symbol, read as a keyword -- names a map
   entry; a lone : selects every entry of a level; and a sequence key is a
   nested path. A scalar is its own sole element, so (get 5 1) is 5. On a
   held form, index 1 is the ' head; hold=true makes a root hold transparent,
   walks the content without reducing, and holds the result. A table keeps
   its own path grammar (row, column, index key). get is overridable: a user
   rule may take precedence, which is how a namespace or a custom value
   defines its own access.")

(docdef :core hold-get
  "(hold-get coll key…; hold=false)
   get with a root hold made transparent: the path is walked over the content
   and the result is held. hold=false returns it unheld instead.")

(docdef :core with
  "(with coll key… value; hold=false)
   A copy of coll with the subform at the path replaced by value. With a : key
   a scalar broadcasts to every entry and a container supplies one value per
   entry (sequence elements first, then map values, in canonical order). On a
   table it replaces a column, updates a row's cells, or sets one cell.")

(docdef :core wout
  "(wout coll key…)
   with-out: a copy of coll with the entry (or every entry, for a : key) at
   the path removed, the structure above it rebuilt.")

(docdef :core nth
  "(nth coll i)
   The i-th sequence element, 1-based, like get with an integer key.")

(docdef :core join
  "(join coll…)
   The arguments concatenated: sequences join elementwise and maps merge,
   left to right.")

(docdef :core join-in
  "(join-in coll key… value)
   join applied at the subform named by the path: the value is joined onto
   that subform.")

(docdef :core push-in
  "(push-in coll key… value…)
   push applied at the subform named by the path.")

(docdef :core update
  "(update coll key f)
   A copy of coll with the entry at key replaced by (f old).")

(docdef :core update-in
  "(update-in coll key… f)
   The deep update: (update-in coll k1 … kn f) replaces the subform at the
   path with (f old), and a missing key is naught to f.")

(docdef :core in
  "(in x coll)
   Whether x is an element of coll, as member? with the arguments reversed for
   pipeline use.")

(docdef :core merge
  "(merge coll…)
   The maps merged left to right, later entries winning; sequence elements
   are concatenated, as join.")

(docdef :core compare
  "(compare a b)
   -1, 0, or 1, ordering two forms by the canonical order (numbers, strings,
   and symbols by value; containers elementwise).")

(docdef :core hash
  "(hash x)
   An integer hash consistent with eq?: equivalent forms hash alike, and a
   mutable contributes only its identity.")

(docdef :core keyword
  "(keyword x)
   A keyword from a string or symbol: (keyword \"host\") is :host.")

(docdef :core symbol
  "(symbol x)
   A symbol from a string or symbol: (symbol \"x\") is x. The text is not
   validated, so (symbol \"?y\") is the wildcard ?y.")

(docdef :core name
  "(name x)
   The text of a keyword, symbol, or wildcard without its marker:
   (name :host) is \"host\" and (name ?x) is \"x\".")

# ---- holds --------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :hold hold
  "(hold e… key=value…)
   The reserved alias for the hold head: it returns the canonical hold
   (' e…) of its arguments, exactly as written. A hold is a container whose
   head is the symbol ' and it is data, not code.")

(docdef :hold unhold
  "(unhold x)
   Remove one hold and evaluate what it held. A bound symbol is resolved
   first, so (unhold a) evaluates the value bound to a -- e.g. a held
   parameter of a def.")

(docdef :hold preval
  "(preval x), or the reader sugar ~x
   The eager hole: it evaluates x wherever it appears. Inside a held form the
   hold's expansion fills a preval in recursively at every depth.")

(docdef :hold lift
  "(lift form)
   Build a held form explicitly: literal leaves become quoted ('a) and
   evaluated elements are written plainly. (lift (' 'a x 'b)) is
   (' a <value-of-x> b).")

(docdef :hold lower
  "(lower form), (lower form n)
   Push holds toward the leaves, n levels deep (default 1). A structural,
   non-evaluating rewrite; lift is its inverse at the root.")

(docdef :hold (held? hold-held?)
  "(held? x), (held? form subindices…)
   Whether x is a held form, or whether the subpart at a path is held.
   hold-held? is the hold- form: it makes the root hold transparent first.")

(docdef :hold (hold-nth hold-with hold-wout hold-push hold-push-in hold-join
               hold-join-in hold-update hold-update-in)
  "(hold-nth coll i), (hold-with coll key… value), (hold-wout coll key…),
   (hold-push coll x…), (hold-push-in coll key… value…),
   (hold-join coll…), (hold-join-in coll key… value),
   (hold-update coll key f), (hold-update-in coll key… f)
   The hold- family: each operation treats a root hold as transparent (it
   operates on the content), treats inserted held values as their content,
   and returns the result held. The plain version accepts hold=true for the
   same treatment, and hold=false on the hold- version unholds the result.")

# ---- control ------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :control (if cond)
  "(if c1 v1 c2 v2 … else)
   The conditional. Evaluate the conditions in order and yield the value paired
   with the first truthy one; a trailing unpaired form is the else value, and
   with no match the result is naught. The selected value is reduced in tail
   position. cond is a synonym.")

(docdef :control let
  "(let (x=1 y=2) body…), (let ((a=1) (b=(a + 1))) body…;
   hold=(…), bind=(…))
   Evaluate the bindings, then substitute each name by its value in the body
   *before* evaluating it. let introduces no scope of its own and its
   substitution is textual, so it reaches inside a held form. One mapping's
   bindings are parallel; a sequence of mappings feeds one into the next. A
   name in hold=(…) is inserted exactly as written instead of evaluated, and a
   name in bind=(…) is installed as a lexical binding instead of substituted.")

(docdef :control do
  "(do options… body… (except pattern body…)… (handle pattern body…)…
   (finally body…))
   The block form. Evaluate the body forms in order and yield the last value;
   the trailing clauses catch errors (except) and signals (handle), and finally
   always runs. Options are let= (a lexical scope around the body), catch=true
   or catch=:tag for (throw …), and :let markers inside the body. A :let
   marker is a nested textual let, invisible to the clauses.")

(docdef :control not
  "(not x)
   The Boolean negation: true when x is falsy, false when it is truthful.")

(docdef :control xor
  "(xor e…)
   True when an odd number of the arguments is truthy.")

(docdef :control case
  "(case subject clause… default?)
   Dispatch by pattern. Each clause is a replacement rule (pattern ->
   replacement);
   the first whose pattern matches the subject wins, and its replacement may use
   _ for the whole subject. A trailing non-rule form is the default, and no match
   at all yields naught. select is a synonym.")

(docdef :control select
  "(select subject clause… default?)
   A synonym for case.")

(docdef :control (when unless)
  "(when cond body…), (unless cond body…)
   Evaluate the body forms when cond is truthy (when) or falsy (unless), and
   yield the last value; otherwise naught.")

(docdef :control map
  "(map f coll depth=n)
   Apply f to every node at depth n (default 1: the collection's elements --
   both sequence elements and map values), rebuilding the structure around the
   results. depth=0 applies f to coll itself. A scalar is its own sole element,
   so (map f 3) is (f 3).")

(docdef :control filter
  "(filter pred coll)
   The elements for which (pred x) is truthy, in order; a table filters rows
   and a tensor its elements.")

(docdef :control remove
  "(remove pred coll)
   The elements for which (pred x) is falsy.")

(docdef :control reduce
  "(reduce f init coll)
   The left fold of f over the sequence elements, starting from init. fold is a
   synonym.")

(docdef :control fold
  "(fold f init coll)
   A synonym for reduce.")

(docdef :control mapcat
  "(mapcat f coll)
   Map f over the elements and join the results, one level deep.")

(docdef :control each
  "(each f coll)
   Apply f to every value for its effect, in order; returns naught. Works on
   sequences, tables (by row), and tensors.")

(docdef :control apply
  "(apply f args-form)
   Build and reduce the call (f a b …) from the elements of args-form.")

(docdef :control for
  "(for (x coll … :if cond :let name val) body…)
   A comprehension. The bindings are processed left to right and nest; :if skips
   an iteration when its condition is falsy and :let binds a name for the rest of
   the binding form. The result is the list of body values.")

(docdef :control doseq
  "(doseq (x coll …) body…)
   Run the body for each iteration of the bindings, for effect; returns naught.")

(docdef :control dotimes
  "(dotimes (i n) body…)
   Run the body with i bound to 0 … n-1; returns naught.")

(docdef :control loop
  "(loop (a 0 b 1 …) body…)
   Bind the name/value pairs, evaluate the body, and if it reaches a (recur …),
   rebind and re-enter. recur in tail position runs in constant stack.")

(docdef :control recur
  "(recur value…)
   Re-enter the enclosing loop with new bindings. It is control flow, not an
   error: no error clause catches it.")

(docdef :control while
  "(while cond body…)
   Evaluate the body while cond is truthy; the result is naught.")

(docdef :control exit
  "(exit [value])
   End the current task with value (default naught). The nearest boundary -- a
   task, a REPL, a loaded file, or an SSH session -- turns it into that
   boundary's result. It is control flow, so no error clause catches it.")

(docdef :control thread
  "(thread form; hold=false)
   Mathematica-style threading: distribute the form over its sequential
   container elements. (thread (f (1 2 3))) is ((f 1) (f 2) (f 3)). The head is
   not special, so (thread (f (' a b))) is ((f ') (f a) (f b)).")

(docdef :control hold-thread
  "(hold-thread form; hold=true)
   thread after stripping the argument's root hold; the result is held again:
   (hold-thread (' f (1 2 3))) is (' (f 1) (f 2) (f 3)).")

(docdef :control >>
  "(>> x step…), or the infix spelling (x >> step…)
   The pipeline. Evaluate x, substitute the result textually for every _ in the
   raw first step, substitute that form into the next step, and reduce only the
   final form. A step must contain a _.")

(docdef :control part
  "(part f a b …)
   A partially applied call: the arguments are held, and applying the result to
   more arguments appends them: ((part f a b) c d) is (f a b c d). A ~arg is
   evaluated first, so its value is captured.")

(docdef :control comp
  "(comp f g …)
   Function composition, right to left: ((comp f g) x) is (f (g x)). The
   arguments are held, as for part.")

(docdef :control juxt
  "(juxt f g …)
   Apply every function to the same arguments and return the results as a list:
   ((juxt inc dec) 3) is (4 2).")

(docdef :control complement
  "(complement f)
   The negation of a predicate: ((complement even?) 3) is true.")

(docdef :control memoize
  "(memoize f)
   A callable that returns the cached result of (f args…). The cache belongs to
   the environment and is keyed by the function and the argument forms, so it is
   not transactional; a def can also be memoized directly with memoize=true.")

(docdef :control memoized
  "(memoized f args-container)
   The internal form (memoize f) applies; not normally called directly.")

(docdef :control eval
  "(eval form)
   Evaluate form as data, in the global scope (no local bindings). A held form
   is unheld first, so (eval (' 1 + 2)) is 3.")

# ---- rules and definitions ----------------------------------------------------

(docdef :rules def
  "(def pattern body…; doc=…, hold=(…), memoize=…, tx=…, let=…,
        catch=…)
   Register a reduction rule: a form matching pattern is replaced by body. At
   the top level the rule is global; inside a (namespace …) block it is appended
   to that namespace. A literal string at the head of the body is the docstring.
   With no body the rule is implicit -- it reproduces its matched form, with its
   arguments reduced -- so the form is a fixed point. The pattern must contain at
   least one raw-symbol anchor, and a raw-symbol head may not name a builtin.")

(docdef :rules val
  "(val pattern form…; doc=…, tx=…, let=…, catch=…)
   Like def, but evaluate form once at definition time and install the value as a
   thread-local constant, backed by an Air Var. A reference to the name yields the
   value; the zero-argument call (.ns.name) is a one-element form containing it.
   bind can shadow the value for a dynamic extent.")

(docdef :rules doc
  "(doc), (doc name), (doc (form …))
   (doc name) lists the builtin catalogue entry for name and every documented
   rule whose pattern uses it; (doc (form …)) holds the argument and lists the
   documented rules whose pattern matches it; (doc) returns the index of
   documented names; (doc .air) is the same index and (doc .air.name) documents
   one builtin. With no argument, see also (help).")

(docdef :rules definitions
  "(definitions name), (definitions (form …))
   The matching def forms as quoted data, with each docstring moved into a doc=
   map entry. It accepts the same argument as doc.")

(docdef :rules match?
  "(match? pattern subject)
   Whether the pattern matches the reduced subject.")

(docdef :rules matches
  "(matches pattern subject)
   The bindings of the match as a mapping, or naught when the pattern does not
   match.")

(docdef :rules replace
  "(replace coll clause… depth=0)
   Apply the replacement rules to the nodes at depth n (default 0: the subject
   itself), rebuilding the structure. Each clause is (pattern -> replacement);
   the first matching clause wins, and _ in a replacement is the whole matched
   subject.")

(docdef :rules replace-all
  "(replace-all coll clause…)
   Apply the replacement rules at every depth of the tree, bottom-up; with
   depth=n, only at that level.")

(docdef :rules rreplace
  "(rreplace coll clause… depth=0)
   replace applied to a held subject: the root hold is transparent and the
   result is held.")

(docdef :rules rreplace-all
  "(rreplace-all coll clause…)
   replace-all applied to a held subject; the result is held.")

# ---- documentation ------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :docs docdef
  "(docdef [category] name… \"text\"…)
   Declare documentation for one or more builtin names, as this catalogue does.
   The category is an optional keyword; the names are raw symbols or a form of
   them; the strings are joined and deindented. The first entry for a name
   fixes its category and place in the index; a later entry amends it, its text
   appended as a further paragraph. A docdef is a process-global registration,
   like layout -- sandbox and transactions do not undo one.")

(docdef :docs help
  "(help), (help name), (help :category)
   (help) prints an introduction to the Air language. (help name) prints the
   documentation for a name, as (doc name) returns it, and (help :category) or
   (help :all) prints the index of documented names.")

# ---- collections --------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :collection (first second third last rest)
  "(first coll), (second coll), (third coll), (last coll), (rest coll)
   Positional access to the sequence elements. A scalar is a singleton, so
   (first 5) is 5, and (rest x) is the form without its first element. These are
   hold-blind: on a held form, (first …) is the ' head.")

(docdef :collection count
  "(count coll)
   The number of sequence elements. A scalar is a singleton (so (count ?g) is 1
   for a one-element group), naught is 0, a table reports its rows, a tensor its
   elements, and a stream its queued deliveries.")

(docdef :collection (take drop-first take-last drop-last)
  "(take coll n), (drop-first coll n), (take-last coll n), (drop-last coll n)
   The first n elements, the form without its first n, the last n, and the form
   without its last n.")

(docdef :collection (take-while drop-while)
  "(take-while pred coll), (drop-while pred coll)
   The leading elements for which pred is truthy, and the elements after that
   prefix.")

(docdef :collection insert
  "(insert coll i x)
   A copy of coll with x inserted before position i (1-based; i = n + 1
   appends). with only appends, so this is the arbitrary-index case.")

(docdef :collection split-at
  "(split-at coll i)
   The pair of the first i elements and the rest: (split-at (a b c d) 2) is
   ((a b) (c d)).")

(docdef :collection partition
  "(partition coll n)
   The elements in groups of n; a final short group is kept.")

(docdef :collection partition-by
  "(partition-by f coll)
   Group ADJACENT elements by the key (f x); a new group begins whenever the
   key changes: (partition-by odd? (1 3 2 4 5)) is ((1 3) (2 4) (5)). split-by
   is a synonym.")

(docdef :collection split-by
  "(split-by f coll)
   A synonym for partition-by.")

(docdef :collection gather-by
  "(gather-by f coll)
   Group ALL elements whose keys (f x) are equal, in the order the keys first
   appear: (gather-by odd? (1 2 3 4)) is ((1 3) (2 4)). Compare partition-by,
   which groups only adjacent elements, and group-by, which returns a map.")

(docdef :collection group-by
  "(group-by f coll)
   A map from each printed key (f x) to the elements that produced it.")

(docdef :collection frequencies
  "(frequencies coll)
   A map from each element, as a keyword, to the number of times it occurs.")

(docdef :collection (distinct dedupe count-distinct)
  "(distinct coll), (dedupe coll), (count-distinct coll)
   distinct drops repeated elements anywhere; dedupe drops only adjacent
   repeats; count-distinct is the number of distinct elements. All three compare
   with eq?.")

(docdef :collection shuffle
  "(shuffle coll)
   The sequence elements in a random order; map entries are kept.")

(docdef :collection reverse
  "(reverse coll)
   The elements in reverse order; for a tensor, a reversed copy along the axes or,
   with dims=, along the named ones.")

(docdef :collection sort
  "(sort coll; key=…, reverse=…)
   A sorted copy of the sequence elements; key= extracts a comparison key, and
   reverse=true sorts descending. A tensor sorts linearly, or along dims=.")

(docdef :collection sort-by
  "(sort-by f coll)
   Sorted by the key (f x), ascending.")

(docdef :collection (keys vals)
  "(keys coll), (vals coll)
   The map's keys (as keywords) and its values (as a sequence).")

(docdef :collection invert
  "(invert coll)
   The map with its keys and values exchanged.")

(docdef :collection zipmap
  "(zipmap ks vs)
   A map pairing each key of ks with the value at the same position in vs.")

(docdef :collection (union intersect difference)
  "(union a b …), (intersect a b …), (difference a b …)
   Set operations over the sequence elements, comparing with eq?. union keeps
   the elements of every argument once, in first-seen order; intersect keeps the
   elements common to all; difference keeps a's elements that are in no later
   argument. A scalar is a singleton.")

(docdef :collection (subset? superset?)
  "(subset? a b), (superset? a b)
   Whether every element of a is an element of b, and the reverse.")

(docdef :collection (flatten)
  "(flatten coll)
   A sequence with the nesting stripped: every nested sequence is spliced in
   place, recursively. Map entries are kept.")

(docdef :collection transpose
  "(transpose rows; perm=…)
   The columns of the rows, stopping at the shortest:
   (transpose ((1 2 3) (4 5 6))) is ((1 4) (2 5) (3 6)). A tensor transposes its
   axes -- a vector becomes a one-row matrix -- and perm= selects the order
   explicitly.")

(docdef :collection (zip)
  "(zip coll …)
   A sequence of argument tuples, stopping at the shortest input:
   (zip (1 2) (3 4)) is ((1 3) (2 4)).")

(docdef :collection interleave
  "(interleave coll …)
   The elements of each input in turn, stopping at the shortest:
   (interleave (1 2) (a b)) is (1 a 2 b).")

(docdef :collection (prepend)
  "(prepend coll x …)
   The sequence elements of coll with x… in front: (prepend (3 4) 1 2) is
   (1 2 3 4).")

(docdef :collection (riffle)
  "(riffle coll sep)
   Insert sep between the elements: (riffle (1 3 5) 0) is (1 0 3 0 5). When sep
   is a collection its elements are used in turn and cycle.")

(docdef :collection (tuples)
  "(tuples lists), (tuples coll n)
   The Cartesian product: (tuples ((a b) (1 2))) is ((a 1) (a 2) (b 1) (b 2)).
   With an integer second argument, the n-fold product of one collection.")

(docdef :collection (outer)
  "(outer f coll …)
   The outer product: f applied to every combination, nested one level per
   collection: (outer * (1 2) (3 4)) is ((3 4) (6 8)).")

(docdef :collection (map-thread)
  "(map-thread f coll …)
   f applied to the i-th element of each collection, stopping at the shortest:
   (map-thread + (1 2) (3 4)) is (4 6).")

(docdef :collection (array)
  "(array f n), (array f n start)
   (f start) … (f (start + n - 1)), counting from start (default 1):
   (array (?x -> (* x x)) 4) is (1 4 9 16).")

(docdef :collection (range)
  "(range n), (range start stop), (range start step stop)
   The integers from 0 to n - 1, from start to stop - 1, or from start by step up
   to stop - 1.")

(docdef :collection (repeat)
  "(repeat n x)
   A sequence of n copies of x.")

(docdef :collection (subdivide)
  "(subdivide n), (subdivide max n), (subdivide min max n)
   n + 1 evenly spaced floats from min (default 0) to max (default 1):
   (subdivide 4) is (0.0 0.25 0.5 0.75 1.0).")

(docdef :collection (sum product)
  "(sum coll), (product coll; dims=…, drop=…)
   The numeric sum (or product) of the collection's elements. On a tensor the
   reduction is Julia's, with dims= and drop= selecting and removing axes;
   non-numeric input is left symbolic.")

(docdef :collection (mean median variance std)
  "(mean coll; dims=…, drop=…), (median coll; dims=…, drop=…),
   (variance coll ddof=1; dims=…, drop=…),
   (std coll ddof=1; dims=…, drop=…)
   Descriptive statistics of the numeric elements, with sample variance by
   default (denominator n - ddof). On a tensor they reduce along dims= and drop=
   applies. A result with no defined value is naught.")

(docdef :collection cases
  "(cases coll clause… depth=1)
   Every element at depth n that matches a clause, in encounter order. A bare
   pattern keeps the element as it is; a replacement rule replaces it. There is
   no default clause, since a bare pattern is indistinguishable from one.")

(docdef :collection drop-cases
  "(drop-cases coll clause… depth=1)
   The elements at depth n that match NO clause, with the structure above them
   rebuilt; a clause may be a bare pattern or a replacement rule.")

(docdef :collection matches-any?
  "(matches-any? subject clause…)
   True when the subject itself or any subpart anywhere in the tree matches a
   clause -- a bare pattern with an optional rule-level guard, or a replacement
   rule, of which only the pattern and guard matter.")

(docdef :collection where
  "(where coll obj depth=d)
   The locations of every element of coll equal to obj. With no depth the depth
   is 1 and the result is the bare keys in encounter order -- (where (:a :b :b)
   :b) is (2 3); with an integer depth it is one path per occurrence -- (where
   ((1 2) (3 2)) 2 depth=2) is ((1 2) (2 2)); depth=:all reports every depth,
   each path of its own length. Depth 0 names the subject itself, whose sole key
   is 0. No match is (). Paths splice into get, with, and update-in.")

(docdef :collection where-all
  "(where-all coll obj)
   Removed. Write (where coll obj depth=:all); calling it raises an error that
   says so.")

(docdef :collection deep-map
  "(deep-map f x)
   f applied to every node of the tree, bottom-up.")

(docdef :collection map-keys
  "(map-keys f m)
   Rebuild m with each key k replaced by (f k); f receives a keyword and must
   return a symbol.")

(docdef :collection trampoline
  "(trampoline f arg…)
   Call f with the arguments; while the result is a function (a replacement
   rule), call that with no arguments. Mutually tail-recursive steps can bounce
   through the trampoline instead of growing the stack.")

# ---- strings ------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :string regex
  "(regex source), (regex source flags)
   A compiled pattern value. Every string verb that takes a pattern accepts
   either a literal string (matched literally) or this value (matched as a
   regular expression). The reader's r\"…\"flags literal builds the same
   value.")

(docdef :string regex?
  "(regex? x)
   Whether x is a compiled regular expression.")

(docdef :string string-length
  "(string-length s)
   The number of characters in s.")

(docdef :string string-join
  "(string-join coll sep)
   The printed elements joined by sep.")

(docdef :string string-append
  "(string-append s …)
   The arguments concatenated as text.")

(docdef :string substring
  "(substring s start end)
   1-based and inclusive, as Julia's s[start:end].")

(docdef :string string-replace
  "(string-replace s pat rep)
   Replace every match of pat in s with rep; a regex rep expands $0, $1, $name,
   and $$.")

(docdef :string string-split
  "(string-split s pat)
   The pieces of s between matches of pat.")

(docdef :string (string-upper upcase string-lower downcase)
  "(string-upper s), (upcase s), (string-lower s), (downcase s)
   Upper- and lower-cased copies of s.")

(docdef :string (string-trim string-trim-left string-trim-right)
  "(string-trim s), (string-trim-left s), (string-trim-right s)
   s with whitespace removed from both ends, the left, or the right.")

(docdef :string (string-take string-drop)
  "(string-take s n), (string-drop s n)
   The first n characters of s, and the rest; n is clamped to the length.")

(docdef :string string-capitalize
  "(string-capitalize s)
   The first character upper-cased and the rest lower-cased.")

(docdef :string (string-starts-with? string-ends-with?)
  "(string-starts-with? s p), (string-ends-with? s p)
   Whether s begins or ends with the text p.")

(docdef :string string-contains?
  "(string-contains? s p)
   Whether p occurs in s; p is literal text.")

(docdef :string string-match?
  "(string-match? s pat)
   The pattern-aware spelling of string-contains?: true when pat matches
   somewhere in s.")

(docdef :string string-index-of
  "(string-index-of s p)
   The 1-based index of the first occurrence of p in s, or naught.")

(docdef :string string-find
  "(string-find s pat)
   The first match as (match=… index=… groups=…), or naught. The pattern is
   literal text or a regex value.")

(docdef :string string-find-all
  "(string-find-all s pat)
   Every non-overlapping match, in order, each as
   (match=… index=… groups=…).")

(docdef :string string-reverse
  "(string-reverse s)
   The characters of s in reverse order.")

(docdef :string string-repeat
  "(string-repeat s n)
   s repeated n times.")

(docdef :string (string-pad-left string-pad-right)
  "(string-pad-left s n), (string-pad-right s n)
   s padded with spaces to length n, on the left or the right.")

(docdef :string string-chars
  "(string-chars s)
   The characters of s as a sequence of one-character strings.")

(docdef :string string-lines
  "(string-lines s)
   The lines of s as a sequence of strings.")

(docdef :string form->string
  "(form->string x pretty=true width=n)
   The source text of a form. The default is the flat spelling; pretty=true
   breaks it across lines to fit width (80 by default).")

(docdef :string string->form
  "(string->form s)
   Read the air source text s into a form.")

(docdef :string string-format
  "(string-format fmt key=value …)
   Format the text fmt, replacing each ~name by the printed value given under
   that map entry; ~~ is a literal tilde.")

# ---- math ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :math (abs abs2 sign sqrt cbrt exp exp2 exp10 expm1 log log2 log10 log1p
               sin cos tan asin acos atan sinh cosh tanh asinh acosh atanh
               sinc sinpi cospi deg2rad rad2deg real imag conj angle cis
               factorial)
  "(abs x), (sqrt x), (exp x), (log x), (sin x), (cos x), (tan x), … along with
   abs2, sign, cbrt, exp2, exp10, expm1, log2, log10, log1p, asin, acos, atan,
   sinh, cosh, tanh, asinh, acosh, atanh, sinc, sinpi, cospi, deg2rad, rad2deg,
   real, imag, conj, angle, cis, and factorial
   The elementary Julia math functions, Julia semantics and promotion included.
   Each takes one argument, broadcasts over a tensor, and stays symbolic on a
   non-number, so (sqrt x) is a fixed point.")

(docdef :math (isnan isinf isfinite isinteger iseven isodd)
  "(isnan x), (isinf x), (isfinite x), (isinteger x), (iseven x), (isodd x)
   Elementwise tests: each yields a Boolean per value, and a Boolean tensor over
   a tensor, unlike the value predicates nan? and even?. Each stays symbolic on
   a non-number.")

(docdef :math (atan2 gcd lcm binomial hypot rem copysign)
  "(atan2 y x), (gcd a b), (lcm a b), (binomial n k), (hypot a b), (rem a b),
   (copysign a b)
   Two-argument Julia math functions. They broadcast over tensors and stay
   symbolic on a non-number.")

(docdef :math (round floor ceil truncate)
  "(round x; digits=…, sigdigits=…), (floor x; digits=…),
   (ceil x; digits=…),
   (truncate x; digits=…)
   Rounding. digits= rounds to that many decimal places and sigdigits= to that
   many significant digits; both are symbolic-preserving options that survive an
   unreduced argument.")

(docdef :math clamp
  "(clamp x lo hi)
   x bounded to the range lo … hi, broadcast over tensors.")

(docdef :math div
  "(div x y)
   Integer division, truncating toward zero.")

(docdef :math rational
  "(rational n d)
   The exact rational n/d.")

(docdef :math complex
  "(complex re im)
   The complex value with the given real and imaginary parts; ComplexF64 when
   both are exactly Float64 and Complex{BigFloat} otherwise.")

(docdef :math bigfloat
  "(bigfloat \"text\" precision=n)
   The BigFloat the text denotes, parsed at precision (default: the ambient
   one). The printer uses this spelling for a value whose precision is not the
   ambient default.")

(docdef :math (min max)
  "(min x …; dims=…, drop=…), (max x …; dims=…, drop=…)
   The least (or greatest) of the arguments; a single collection argument is
   searched elementwise, and on a tensor dims= and drop= select the reduction
   axes. Non-numeric input stays symbolic.")

(docdef :math (bit-and bit-or bit-xor)
  "(bit-and a b …), (bit-or a b …), (bit-xor a b …)
   Bitwise and, or, and exclusive or; a Boolean counts as an integer, so
   (bit-and true 1) is 1.")

(docdef :math bit-not
  "(bit-not x)
   The bitwise complement.")

(docdef :math (bit-shift-left bit-shift-right)
  "(bit-shift-left x n), (bit-shift-right x n)
   x shifted left or right by n bits.")

# ---- operators ----------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :operator (+ - * /)
  "(+ a b …), (- a b …), (* a b …), (/ a b …)
   Arithmetic. The canonical form is infix -- (a + b) -- and the prefix spelling
   (OP a b c) folds left, so (+ a b c) is ((a + b) + c). +, -, and * are Julia's
   arithmetic with promotion; / is always floating point. A tensor operand
   broadcasts. The rules match numbers, so a non-numeric operand stays symbolic:
   (x + 1) is a fixed point.")

(docdef :operator (^ // mod)
  "(^ a b), (a // b), (a mod b)
   Exponentiation, the exact rational constructor, and the modulus. Like the
   other operators they stay symbolic on a non-number.")

(docdef :operator (== != === !== < > <= >=)
  "(== a b), (!= a b), (=== a b), (!== a b), (< a b), (> a b), (<= a b),
   (>= a b)
   Comparison. == is content equality (numeric-friendly: (== 1 1.0) is true);
   === is eq?, equivalence that distinguishes 1 from 1.0; != and !== are their
   negations; the ordering operators compare numbers, strings, symbols, and
   times, and stay symbolic otherwise. On a tensor the ordering operators are
   symbolic too -- use tensor-lt and friends for elementwise comparison.")

(docdef :operator (and or)
  "(a and b), (a or b), (and e …), (or e …)
   Short-circuiting conjunction and disjunction. Infix is binary; the prefix
   spelling folds a whole run, and and yields the first falsy value or the
   last, or the first truthy value or naught. (and) is true and (or) is
   naught. Only true is truthy.")

# ---- time ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :time now
  "(now)
   The current instant, as the ordinary form (time SECONDS) of UTC epoch
   seconds at millisecond precision.")

(docdef :time time
  "(time x)
   The value of x as a time: a number is UTC epoch seconds, an ISO-8601 string
   is parsed, and a time is already one. Anything else stays symbolic.")

(docdef :time time?
  "(time? x)
   Whether x is a time form.")

(docdef :time time-str
  "(time-str t; format=\"…\", offset=\"+02:00\")
   The text of a time. The default is ISO-8601 UTC with milliseconds; format is
   a Julia Dates.DateFormat string; offset shifts the instant and replaces the
   Z suffix.")

(docdef :time (before? after? between?)
  "(before? a b), (after? a b), (between? x lo hi)
   Time comparisons; a number contributes its epoch seconds, and each stays
   symbolic on a non-value.")

# ---- input and output ---------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :io print
  "(print x …)
   Write the arguments to the task's output sink -- air's actor-backed stdout,
   or the SSH session's channel when the task runs in one -- and return naught. A
   string prints as its content and anything else as air source. The write is a
   side effect: inside a transaction it happens at commit.")

(docdef :io println
  "(println x …)
   print followed by a newline.")

(docdef :io str
  "(str x …)
   The arguments concatenated into a string, as print renders them.")

(docdef :io read-file
  "(read-file path)
   The file's contents as a string. Requires the filesystem permission on
   .air.sys; a relative path resolves against the directory of the file being
   loaded.")

(docdef :io write-file
  "(write-file path text)
   Write text to the file, replacing it. Requires the filesystem permission; a
   write inside a transaction is deferred to its commit and dropped on abort.")

# ---- data formats -------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :format decode
  "(decode text format=:json)
   Parse a string or a bytes value into a form, as plain data: an object is a
   map form, an array a sequence form, and null or an empty container is naught.
   A format is :json, :yaml, :toml, :csv, :tsv, or :env. Nothing is constructed
   or executed -- the YAML reader is a safe subset and unknown tags are a
   :toml-date/:toml-time/:toml-datetime marker form or an error.")

(docdef :format encode
  "(encode value format=:json)
   Render a form as text in the given data format. Only plain data has a
   representation: an extern, a mutable, a rational, a complex, or a container
   with both sequence elements and map entries raises.")

(docdef :format read-data
  "(read-data path format=…)
   Read and decode a data file, inferring the format from its extension (.json,
   .yaml, .yml, .toml, .csv, .tsv, or the file name .env). Requires the
   filesystem permission.")

(docdef :format write-data
  "(write-data path value format=…)
   Encode a form and write it to a file, inferring the format from the
   extension; the write is deferred to the transaction commit.")

# ---- errors -------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :error error
  "(error tag data … key=value …)
   An inert error object: the error head never reduces, so its elements stay
   raw at any depth and an except clause can match them structurally. tag is a
   keyword by convention only -- air never inspects it.")

(docdef :error raise
  "(raise tag data …; from=…, cause=…)
   Evaluate the arguments and throw (error tag data …). A single argument that
   already is an error object is thrown exactly. from= overrides (or, with
   naught, suppresses) the automatic chain to the error being handled, and
   cause= is explicit context.")

(docdef :error signal
  "(signal tag data …)
   An inert signal object, dispatched by notify to the innermost matching handle
   clause rather than thrown.")

(docdef :error trace
  "(trace body …)
   Evaluate the body and yield its value; if an error escapes, yield the data
   form (:trace (error …) frames=(…)) instead of throwing -- the first element
   is the object an except would match and frames lists the source frames,
   innermost first.")

# ---- testing ------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :test check
  "(check expr)
   Evaluate expr; inside a (test …) body a truthy value records a pass and a
   falsy one a failure. Outside one it is simply a truth test. Returns a
   Boolean.")

(docdef :test test
  "(test name body …)
   Evaluate the body with a fresh counter; each check is recorded, and an
   uncaught error is recorded as one failure without aborting the caller. Yields
   (name=… pass=… fail=… failures=(…)) with each failure held.")

# ---- namespaces ---------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :namespace namespace
  "(namespace path body …; using=(…) …)
   Evaluate body in a transaction with path as the current namespace; every def
   and val in it is appended there, and subnamespaces are created as needed. The
   body does not inherit the caller's definitions -- it runs with the namespace's
   rules -- while lexical bindings remain visible.")

(docdef :namespace using
  "(using spec … body …)
   Activate one namespace, or a list of them, for body: its rules match and its
   as= bindings are in scope. Nothing is copied. A spec may carry only=(…),
   except=(…), or as=sym.")

(docdef :namespace use
  "(use spec …)
   Inside a namespace block, materialize the named entries as private imports: a
   readable def is copied, a readable val shares the source value, and an
   execute-only entry becomes a lazy proxy. Each entry records source=.")

(docdef :namespace import
  "(import spec …)
   As use, but the imported names are exported from the enclosing namespace too.")

(docdef :namespace unuse
  "(unuse ns), (unuse ns name …)
   Remove the current block's imports from ns -- all of them, or just the listed
   names.")

(docdef :namespace (clear del)
  "(clear name), (clear :all)
   Remove the current block's definitions that use name, or every definition and
   import for :all. Outside a namespace block it targets the global rule store.
   del is an alias.")

(docdef :namespace bind
  "(bind (x=value …) body …)
   Rebind the vals named by the keys of the mapping for the dynamic extent of
   body. Each name resolves to the val its namespace context matches; a name
   that is not a val raises. The bindings are task-local and are undone when the
   body finishes.")

(docdef :namespace bindings
  "(bindings)
   The current task's val overrides as a mapping that bind can reinstall.")

(docdef :namespace ns-entries
  "(ns-entries [target] src=false)
   The readable entries of a namespace, in append order, each as
   (entry name= kind= owner= doc= src=) with kind one of :def, :val, :sub,
   :proxy, or :alias. Unreadable and hidden entries are omitted.")

(docdef :namespace ns-entry
  "(ns-entry [target] name src=false)
   One namespace entry, or naught when it is absent or unreadable.")

(docdef :namespace ns-proxy-apply
  "(ns-proxy-apply path name args-container)
   The generated body of a proxied call rule: apply the source namespace's rules
   for name. Not normally written by hand.")

(docdef :namespace ns-vals
  "(ns-vals [target])
   The readable val names of a namespace (default: the current one), newest
   definition last, as a list of keywords: (:val1 :val2). A name redefined in
   the namespace appears once, at its last position.")

(docdef :namespace ns-defs
  "(ns-defs [target])
   The readable def rules of a namespace (default: the current one), each as
   the rule's definition form held and with def replaced by ':
   ((' def (twice ?x) (* x 2))). Any stored source position (src=) is
   stripped; a docstring survives as a doc= entry.")

(docdef :namespace ns-facts
  "(ns-facts [target])
   The base facts of a namespace's implicit logic set (default: the current
   one), in the same held list (facts target) returns: (' (p 1) (q 2)), or
   (') when the set is empty. Needs the readfact permission.")

(docdef :namespace ns-rules
  "(ns-rules [target])
   The rules of a namespace's implicit logic set (default: the current one), in
   the same held list (rules target) returns: (' (rule …) …), or (') when the
   set is empty. Needs the readrule permission.")

# ---- users and permissions ----------------------------------------------------

(docdef :user current-user
  "(current-user)
   The user the current task is running as.")

(docdef :user owner
  "(owner)
   The owner of the def currently being evaluated, else the current user.")

(docdef :user (users agents)
  "(users), (agents)
   The user records the caller may see, and the agent accounts among them.")

(docdef :user user-kind
  "(user-kind id)
   The account kind of a visible user: :human (the default) or :agent.")

(docdef :user as-user
  "(as-user id body …)
   Evaluate body as id, with that user's home as the current namespace. air may
   assume anyone, an admin itself and its constituents, and any user itself. The
   switch is dynamic and is inherited by a task created inside it.")

(docdef :user create-user
  "(create-user name)
   Create a core user with a home. Only air may do this.")

(docdef :user create-constituent
  "(create-constituent name)
   An admin creates its constituent admin:name. air creates air:name; to make
   game:bob, run this as :game.")

(docdef :user set-admin
  "(set-admin id flag)
   Make a core user an admin, or revoke it. Only air may do this, and a
   constituent can never have constituents of its own.")

(docdef :user env
  "(env)
   The current task's environment mapping -- the .env value it sees.")

(docdef :user with-env
  "(with-env mapping body …)
   Merge mapping into .env for the dynamic extent of body and for any task
   created there.")

(docdef :permission permissions
  "(permissions target)
   The target's owner and ACL as a mapping.")

(docdef :permission chmod!
  "(chmod! target read=(…) write=(…) exec=(…) assume=(…) hide=(…)
           anchor=…)
   Replace the listed permission mappings. Needs write on the target. A metadata
   edit waits for its database write to land before returning.")

(docdef :permission chown!
  "(chown! target user)
   Give the target a new owner. Needs write on the target; air may always do it.")

(docdef :permission assume
  "(assume body …)
   Evaluate body with current-user set to the owner of the def being evaluated.
   Legal only inside a definition whose entry grants assume to the caller, so an
   owner can expose a controlled operation on its data.")

# ---- the database -------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :database open-airdb
  "(open-airdb name option …)
   Open or create a database -- memory, url=, or dbname=/user=/… -- root name in
   it, and attach @ to that root for the current task. Namespaces at or under the
   root live in that database.")

(docdef :database close-airdb
  "(close-airdb name)
   Flush and close the database, remove the namespace, and detach @ when it
   pointed at that root.")

(docdef :database named-form
  "(named-form name create=… value=… history=…)
   Open or create the named volatile registered under name in the current
   database. A name is a keyword, symbol, or string normalized to its text;
   air/ is reserved.")

(docdef :database name-form!
  "(name-form! name volatile)
   Point name at an existing volatile. A free name is created like named-form;
   overwriting an existing alias needs write on the form currently named.")

(docdef :database named-forms
  "(named-forms)
   The readable, non-internal names in the current database, as a mapping from
   keyword name to volatile.")

(docdef :database sync
  "(sync)
   A FIFO flush barrier: wait until every write committed before the call has
   been flushed. Call it outside an open transaction to cover that transaction's
   writes.")

(docdef :database status
  "(status)
   (pending=N error=naught): the write queue's state.")

(docdef :database form-id
  "(form-id volatile)
   The volatile's UUID as a string.")

(docdef :database form-at
  "(form-at id)
   The volatile with that UUID in the current database; an unknown id yields a
   handle that fails when it is read.")

(docdef :database loaded?
  "(loaded? volatile)
   Whether the volatile's body has been read into memory.")

(docdef :database history
  "(history volatile)
   Whether the volatile records its history.")

(docdef :database to-json
  "(to-json value)
   The value as JSON text. A volatile becomes a bare \"@uuid\" link, so cycles
   encode; a non-finite float is the marked string \"#inf\", \"#-inf\", or
   \"#nan\".")

(docdef :database from-json
  "(from-json text)
   Read a form, resolving \"@uuid\" links through the current database.")

# ---- logic sets ---------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :logic assert
  "(assert [target] fact; reason=…)
   Assert a fact into the target -- a namespace or a logic-set handle (default:
   the enclosing logic block, else the current namespace). A reason= label is
   recorded with the fact. Needs the target's writefact permission.")

(docdef :logic retract
  "(retract [target] fact; reason=…)
   Retract a base fact from the target. Needs writefact.")

(docdef :logic revise
  "(revise [target] condition changes; reason=…)
   Rewrite the base facts selected by condition: its wildcards name the
   positions and the mapping gives their new values. Only base facts can be
   revised.")

(docdef :logic revise-fact
  "(revise-fact [target] old new; reason=…)
   Replace one base fact by another, both written out.")

(docdef :logic query
  "(query [target] pattern …)
   Every distinct satisfier of a rule-condition block over the target's facts
   and rules, as a plain list of (x=…) mappings; rule-headed patterns search
   rules, everything else searches facts. naught when nothing satisfies.")

(docdef :logic facts
  "(facts target)
   The target's facts as one held list, (' ) when empty.")

(docdef :logic rules
  "(rules target)
   The target's rules as one held list, (' ) when empty.")

(docdef :logic why
  "(why target fact)
   Every reason recorded for a fact: explicit base labels and the
   (because rule premises…) provenance of each derivation, deduplicated. A
   namespace target needs both the fact and the rule read permission.")

(docdef :logic logic
  "(logic target body …)
   Evaluate body with target as the target of any bare or same-targeted logic
   operation, collecting them into one event. Reads inside the body see the set
   as it was on entry; the edits apply together at the end.")

(docdef :logic logic-set
  "(logic-set name)
   Create or open a standalone named logic set and return its handle. Opening
   needs read; mutating needs write; querying needs read.")

(docdef :logic defrule
  "(defrule conditions … instructions …; reason=…)
   Assert a raw (rule …) into the active target without reducing the rule's
   arguments.")

(docdef :logic ingest
  "(ingest source)
   Copy a namespace's or logic set's base facts and rules into the active target
   in one transaction, then saturate. The permissions it will need are checked
   before anything mutates, so an ingest is all-or-nothing.")

(docdef :logic because
  "(because rule premises …)
   The reason form of a derivation: the rule, a keyword label, or naught, then
   the premise facts.")

(docdef :logic rule?
  "(rule? x)
   Whether x is a logic-engine rule form.")

(docdef :logic depend
  "(depend …)
   Reserved for live propagation between logic sets; a tombstone.")

# ---- mutables, transactions, and hooks ----------------------------------------

(docdef :mut mut
  "(mut), (mut value filter=… finalize=…)
   A new mutable holding the (reduced, filtered) value, owned by the creating
   user. A filter runs on every write and its result is stored; a finalizer runs
   after the body of the writing transaction, before the commit attempt. Both are
   kept as written in the mutable's metadata: a replacement rule is applied as a
   function, any other form is evaluated with _ bound to the value.")

(docdef :mut set!
  "(set! mutable value), (set! form path … value)
   Write a mutable. The path form edits the deepest mutable found at the path.
   A write inside a transaction joins it and is dropped on abort.")

(docdef :mut swap!
  "(swap! mutable fn arg …)
   Atomically replace the mutable's value with (fn value arg …) and return the
   new value: the read, the call, and the write are one transaction, so a
   concurrent writer causes a retry rather than a lost update. alter! is an
   alias.")

(docdef :mut alter!
  "(alter! mutable fn arg …)
   A synonym for swap!.")

(docdef :mut tx
  "(tx body …)
   Evaluate the block inside one STM transaction and yield its last value. It
   accepts the whole do grammar. Air's transactions join rather than nest, so the
   effective boundary is the outermost tx; an escaping error aborts its writes,
   and an error caught by the block's own except commits.")

(docdef :mut sandbox
  "(sandbox body …)
   Evaluate the block in a transaction that is discarded instead of committed:
   the body's value is returned and every write is dropped, so a def, val, mut,
   fact, rule, or namespace created inside is gone afterwards. It runs on a
   private task, so it reads committed state rather than an enclosing
   transaction's staged writes; non-database side effects still happen.")

(docdef :mut watch
  "(watch mutable key fn)
   Register or replace the watch key. fn is kept as written and called as
   (fn key mutable old new) as its registrar, at the end of the outermost
   transaction body. Needs both watch and read on the mutable.")

(docdef :mut unwatch
  "(unwatch mutable key …)
   Remove the named watches, or all of them.")

(docdef :mut watches
  "(watches mutable)
   The watch table, held so that evaluating the result does not run it.")

(docdef :mut set-filter!
  "(set-filter! mutable form)
   Replace the mutable's filter; the next write applies the new one.")

(docdef :mut set-finalize!
  "(set-finalize! mutable form)
   Replace the mutable's finalizer.")

# ---- reference values ----------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :reference promise
  "(promise)
   A new, undelivered promise in the current namespace's database. A form that
   holds one serializes as a link, whether pending or delivered.")

(docdef :reference deliver
  "(deliver p value)
   Fill a promise -- a database promise (the kind task returns) or an ephemeral
   promise extern. Single assignment: a second delivery raises.")

(docdef :reference await
  "(await x)
   Block until the promise is ready and return its value; a failed task's promise
   rethrows the task's error. Reading an undelivered promise inside a transaction
   raises instead of waiting.")

(docdef :reference ready?
  "(ready? x)
   Whether a promise has been delivered or a delay already forced.")

(docdef :reference task
  "(task form …; clean=false, bindings=…, durable=…, name=…)
   Hold the forms, evaluate them on another task, and return a database-backed
   promise immediately. The forms capture the enclosing lexical scope by
   substitution, and the task starts with the caller's val bindings, identity,
   namespace, @, and .env. durable=true name=x registers a durable job instead,
   which reruns from the start after a restart (see checkpoint).")

(docdef :reference delay
  "(delay body …)
   A lazy value: the body is captured like a task's and stored, and the first get
   evaluates it and replaces the thunk with the result, durably. Immutable, and
   never forced by printing or serialization.")

(docdef :reference actor
  "(actor value history=true)
   A sequential worker whose every value is persisted in the current namespace's
   database. send a function to update it; reset! overwrites it.")

(docdef :reference send
  "(send actor fn)
   Send fn to the actor; it receives the current value and returns the new one.
   Inside a transaction the send is deferred to the commit.")

(docdef :reference reset!
  "(reset! actor value)
   Replace an actor's value without a function.")

(docdef :reference (actor? promise? delay?)
  "(actor? x), (promise? x), (delay? x)
   Whether x is an actor, a promise, or a delay.")

(docdef :reference extern
  "(extern :type payload)
   Rebuild the extern registered under :type from payload, so a printed extern
   reads back as itself -- a regex, for example.")

(docdef :reference extern?
  "(extern? x)
   Whether x is an extern value.")

(docdef :reference extern-type
  "(extern-type x)
   The registered type name of an extern, as a keyword.")

(docdef :reference future
  "(future …)
   A tombstone: future was renamed to task. A future value still reads with
   await.")

# ---- streams -------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :stream stream
  "(stream maxsize=n)
   A new stream in the current namespace's database: a reusable FIFO of
   deliveries. maxsize bounds the queue; without it the stream is unbounded.")

(docdef :stream post
  "(post s value; timeout=n)
   Append value, stamped with the posting user and instant, and return the
   envelope (obj sender=… time=…). Needs write; naught when a timeout= expired
   on a full stream.")

(docdef :stream fetch
  "(fetch s meta=false; timeout=n)
   Remove and return the oldest object, or the whole envelope with meta=true.
   Needs read; blocks until an item arrives unless timeout= bounds the wait, in
   which case an expiry is naught.")

(docdef :stream (stream? open?)
  "(stream? x), (open? s)
   Whether x is a stream, and whether the stream is still open.")

(docdef :stream close!
  "(close! s)
   Stop the stream. A later post raises (error :stream-closed); a fetch drains
   what remains and then raises it too. Needs write.")

# ---- tables --------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :table table
  "(table (name=\"a\" age=1) …; columns=…, types=…, na=…, index=…),
   (table name=(\"a\" \"b\") age=(1 2))
   Build an immutable table: column-major, ordered names, per-column type
   (:int :float :number :string :bool :symbol :keyword :naught :any) and in-band
   NA marker, and an optional unique index column. Missing is in band: a cell
   equal to its column's NA is missing, and the default marker is NaN for
   :float/:number and NA for every other type.")

(docdef :table table?
  "(table? x)
   Whether x is a table.")

(docdef :table columns
  "(columns t)
   The table's column names, as keywords, in table order.")

(docdef :table schema
  "(schema t)
   The table's column metadata: per-column type, NA marker, and so on.")

(docdef :table nrows
  "(nrows t)
   The number of rows.")

(docdef :table table-index
  "(table-index t)
   The name of the unique index column, or naught.")

# ---- bytes ---------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :bytes bytes
  "(bytes x), (bytes)
   An immutable byte string: from a string, from a sequence of integers, or
   empty. Content-comparing and printable as #<bytes N>; it persists in a
   database but does not survive to-json/from-json text.")

(docdef :bytes bytes?
  "(bytes? x)
   Whether x is a bytes value.")

(docdef :bytes bytes-length
  "(bytes-length b)
   The number of octets.")

(docdef :bytes bytes-string
  "(bytes-string b)
   The octets as a string. This is not a UTF-8 decode: the string carries the
   bytes exactly as they are.")

(docdef :bytes bytes-index
  "(bytes-index b i)
   One octet, 1-based.")

(docdef :bytes bytes-slice
  "(bytes-slice b start stop)
   The octets from start to stop inclusive, as a new bytes value; a slice past
   the end raises.")

# ---- tensors -------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :tensor tensor
  "(tensor coll), (tensor coll eltype=…), or the literal [1 2; 3 4]
   Build an N-dimensional tensor from a nested sequence, or copy a tensor. Cells
   are the numeric-tower leaves, with Julia promotion; [] is an empty Float64
   vector and eltype= names a tower type.")

(docdef :tensor tensor-literal
  "(tensor-literal cells …)
   The reader's [1 2 3] / [1 2; 3 4] spelling: a bracketed literal builds a
   tensor directly from its nested cells. Not normally called by hand.")

(docdef :tensor tensor?
  "(tensor? x)
   Whether x is a tensor.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-shape tensor-rank tensor-eltype)
  "(tensor-shape t), (tensor-rank t), (tensor-eltype t)
   A tensor's dimensions as a sequence, its number of axes, and the numeric-tower
   type of its cells.")

(docdef :tensor tensor-copy
  "(tensor-copy t)
   A fresh, independent copy of the tensor.")

(docdef :tensor tensor->form
  "(tensor->form t)
   The tensor as an ordinary nested form -- the persistence spelling, since a
   tensor itself is unstorable.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-zeros tensor-ones tensor-fill tensor-eye)
  "(tensor-zeros dims …), (tensor-ones dims …), (tensor-fill dims value),
   (tensor-eye n)
   Constructors: an all-zero tensor of the given shape, all ones, filled with a
   value, and the n×n identity matrix.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-rand tensor-randn)
  "(tensor-rand dims …), (tensor-randn dims …)
   A tensor of uniform, or standard-normal, random values of the given shape.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-arange tensor-linspace tensor-randint)
  "(tensor-arange start stop step), (tensor-linspace start stop n),
   (tensor-randint low high dims …)
   Sequence constructors: evenly spaced values from start to stop, and a tensor
   of uniform random integers in low … high.")

(docdef :tensor (reserve-read reserve reservation?)
  "(reserve-read (r …) body …), (reserve (r …) read=(…) body …),
   (reservation? x)
   The reader's {r … : body …} is reserve-read and {r … :! body …} is
   reserve.
   reserve-read takes read locks, runs the body inline, and returns its value;
   reserve takes write locks, runs the body on a spawned task, and returns a
   reservation whose get/await waits for the value. Every access to an existing
   tensor needs an enclosing reservation; reservations never nest and never run
   in a sandbox.")

(docdef :tensor (get set!)
  "(get t key …), (set! t key … value)
   A tensor path: an integer per axis drops the axis, : keeps it, a sequence of
   integers keeps it and selects several, 0 alone is the tensor itself and :
   alone a copy, and default= covers out-of-range. set! writes a cell, a region,
   or the whole tensor.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-map tensor-map! tensor-clamp!)
  "(tensor-map f t), (tensor-map! f t), (tensor-clamp! t lo hi)
   Map a function over every cell, linearly in column-major order. tensor-map
   returns a new tensor; tensor-map! and tensor-clamp! mutate in place, promoting
   the element type as needed.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-mul tensor-add! tensor-sub! tensor-mul! tensor-div!)
  "(tensor-mul a b), (tensor-add! a b), (tensor-sub! a b), (tensor-mul! a b),
   (tensor-div! a b)
   tensor-mul is the elementwise product with scalar/tensor broadcasting; the
   -! verbs mutate their first argument in place.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-scale! tensor-copyto!)
  "(tensor-scale! t factor), (tensor-copyto! dest src)
   Scale every cell in place, and copy one tensor's cells into another.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-equal? tensor-close?)
  "(tensor-equal? a b), (tensor-close? a b rtol=… atol=…)
   Content equality: exact, and within a tolerance.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-lt tensor-le tensor-gt tensor-ge tensor-eq tensor-ne)
  "(tensor-lt a b), (tensor-le a b), (tensor-gt a b), (tensor-ge a b),
   (tensor-eq a b), (tensor-ne a b)
   Elementwise comparison yielding a Boolean tensor. The ordering operators stay
   symbolic and == is identity on a tensor, so these are the comparison verbs.")

(docdef :tensor tensor-where
  "(tensor-where cond a b)
   Elementwise selection: a cell from a where cond is true and from b
   elsewhere.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-concat tensor-stack)
  "(tensor-concat a b …), (tensor-stack a b …)
   Join tensors along an existing axis, and stack them along a new one.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-flip tensor-roll tensor-tile)
  "(tensor-flip t), (tensor-flip t dims), (tensor-roll t shift dims),
   (tensor-tile t reps)
   Reverse, roll, and tile along the named axes.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-squeeze tensor-expand-dims)
  "(tensor-squeeze t), (tensor-expand-dims t dims …)
   Drop length-1 axes, and insert length-1 axes at the named positions.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-diag tensor-diagm tensor-tril tensor-triu)
  "(tensor-diag t), (tensor-diagm t), (tensor-tril t k=-1), (tensor-triu t k=0)
   Diagonal access and extraction, and the lower and upper triangular parts.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-argsort tensor-argmax tensor-argmin)
  "(tensor-argsort t), (tensor-argmax t), (tensor-argmin t)
   Linear (column-major) indices: the sorting permutation, and the position of
   the largest or smallest cell.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-cumsum tensor-cumprod tensor-diff)
  "(tensor-cumsum t), (tensor-cumprod t), (tensor-diff t)
   Cumulative sums and products, and successive differences, along the last
   axis.")

(docdef :tensor (tensor-any tensor-all tensor-count)
  "(tensor-any t), (tensor-all t), (tensor-count t; dims=…, drop=…)
   Reductions over Boolean tensors: whether any cell is true, every cell is
   true, and how many are, with dims= and drop= selecting and removing axes.")

(docdef :tensor (dot norm tr det inv)
  "(dot a b), (norm t), (tr t), (det t), (inv t)
   The LinearAlgebra wrappers: the dot/inner product (conjugating the first
   argument), the norm, the trace, the determinant, and the inverse.")

(docdef :tensor (matrix-rank matrix-cond)
  "(matrix-rank t), (matrix-cond t)
   The rank, and the condition number. cond is air's conditional form, so the
   condition number is matrix-cond.")

(docdef :tensor (linsolve)
  "(linsolve A b)
   The solution of A x = b.")

(docdef :tensor (adjoint reshape)
  "(adjoint t), (reshape t dims …)
   The conjugate transpose (a vector becomes a 1×n row), and a reshaped copy.")

(docdef :tensor (eigen svd qr cholesky lu)
  "(eigen t), (svd t), (qr t), (cholesky t), (lu t)
   The LinearAlgebra factorizations, as forms of their parts.")

(docdef :tensor (eigvals svdvals)
  "(eigvals t), (svdvals t)
   The eigenvalues, and the singular values.")

(docdef :tensor (pinv nullspace)
  "(pinv t), (nullspace t)
   The Moore-Penrose pseudoinverse, and a basis of the null space.")

(docdef :tensor (cross kron)
  "(cross a b), (kron a b)
   The vector cross product, and the Kronecker product.")

(docdef :tensor (isposdef issymmetric ishermitian)
  "(isposdef t), (issymmetric t), (ishermitian t)
   Matrix-shape predicates.")

# ---- the user interface --------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :ui ui
  "(ui kind parameter … child …)
   An inert widget tree: ui is a reserved head, so a (ui …) form never reduces
   at any depth and is ordinary data. Kinds cover Boolean, text, numeric,
   selection, action, display, and layout widgets; the common parameters are id,
   label, description, and disabled, and a stateful kind takes initval,
   on-change, and assert.")

(docdef :ui ui-open
  "(ui-open [key] tree)
   Instantiate a widget tree as a named, owner-scoped instance and register it;
   returns the key (generated when absent).")

(docdef :ui ui-show
  "(ui-show key)
   Attach this SSH session's full-screen renderer to the instance and block
   until it is dismissed (Esc yields naught, Ctrl-C raises (error :ctrl-c)).")

(docdef :ui ui-run
  "(ui-run tree)
   Open, show, and close in one call, returning the final values.")

(docdef :ui (ui-values ui-value)
  "(ui-values key), (ui-value key id)
   Every element's current value, and one element's value.")

(docdef :ui ui-set!
  "(ui-set! key id value)
   A programmatic update. An invalid value changes nothing; the raise is
   (error :ui-invalid id reason).")

(docdef :ui ui-update!
  "(ui-update! key tree)
   Replace an instance's tree; elements whose id survives keep a value that is
   still valid, removed elements' facts are retracted, and new ones asserted.")

(docdef :ui (ui-dismiss! ui-close!)
  "(ui-dismiss! key), (ui-close! key)
   Leave the renderer (ui-show returns), and retract the instance and forget it.
   ui-close! is idempotent.")

(docdef :ui ui-instances
  "(ui-instances)
   The keys of the caller's open instances.")

(docdef :ui current-ui
  "(current-ui)
   The root widget form the current evaluation is reached through -- a
   connection's default UI, or the shown widget inside one. Storing it keeps the
   element and its state alive.")

(docdef :ui ui-render
  "(ui-render key width=80)
   The headless renderer: the instance's current frame as deterministic text.")

(docdef :ui (ui-attach! ui-detach!)
  "(ui-attach! [conn] widget), (ui-detach! [conn])
   Attach a widget to a connection, and detach it. Attaching a raw (ui …) form
   makes an ephemeral projection; attaching an open instance keys it.")

(docdef :ui ui-attached
  "(ui-attached [conn])
   The widget attached to a connection: the form of a projection, or the key of
   an instance.")

(docdef :ui (ui-connection ui-connections)
  "(ui-connection [conn]), (ui-connections [user])
   One connection record, and the live connections the caller may see.")

(docdef :ui (ui-theme ui-theme! ui-style)
  "(ui-theme), (ui-theme! role=entry … [theme] reset=true), (ui-style role)
   The widget theme. ui-theme returns the palette as (theme role=entry …);
   ui-theme! sets roles process-wide, or installs a saved palette; ui-style
   returns one role's style. An entry is a bare fg color or a map of fg=, bg=,
   bold=, dim=, italic=, underline=, and reverse= changes.")

(docdef :ui (ui-code ui-editor ui-notebook)
  "(ui-code [ns] text= state=), (ui-editor [ns] target=name text= state=),
   (ui-notebook [ns] text= state= collapse-lines=20)
   The code widgets: an editable source buffer, the definition editor (Ctrl-J
   appends the buffer's definitions to ns), and a column of input cells with
   their outputs (Ctrl-J runs a cell, Ctrl-R runs them all).")

(docdef :ui ui-browser
  "(ui-browser [root] root= state=)
   The namespace tree, over the current namespace by default.")

(docdef :ui ui-shell
  "(ui-shell state=)
   A tab bar whose tabs are widget forms; a fresh shell starts with a browser and
   a scratch notebook in the current namespace.")

(docdef :ui ui-chat
  "(ui-chat in=s out=s peer=:alice state=)
   A conversation over a pair of streams the caller wires. A draft beginning with
   ( is code mode: it is evaluated in the chat's namespace and only the result is
   posted.")

# ---- agents --------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :agent create-agent
  "(create-agent :bot provider=… model=… api-key=… …)
   Create an :agent constituent of the caller and store its inference settings in
   $.config.agent: provider, dialect, base-url, model, API key, system prompt,
   max-tokens, params, context, compact-at, compact-keep, timeout, and retries.")

(docdef :agent agent?
  "(agent? x)
   Whether x names an agent account.")

(docdef :agent (agent-config agent-config!)
  "(agent-config agent secrets=false), (agent-config! agent key=value …)
   The stored inference settings (agent-config redacts api-key unless
   secrets=true), and an update of the listed settings. Rotating the key affects
   open sessions because it is read live.")

(docdef :agent open-session
  "(open-session agent option …)
   Create an agent session, or reopen one by id=. The session is a durable
   mutable behind the named form agent/<agent>/sessions/<sid>.")

(docdef :agent sessions
  "(sessions agent)
   Every session of the agent, by id.")

(docdef :agent (session-info session-history)
  "(session-info s), (session-history s limit=n)
   A session's status, configuration, and counters, and its transcript (or its
   last n entries).")

(docdef :agent (session-post session-fetch)
  "(session-post s value), (session-fetch s meta=false timeout=n)
   Send a message to the agent, and receive the oldest reply. Use timeout= on
   every blocking fetch.")

(docdef :agent (session-hook! session-channel)
  "(session-hook! s form), (session-channel s)
   Install the on-receive hook -- called as (form msg) with a copy of the
   message, in the namespace where it was installed -- and the session's channel
   handle.")

(docdef :agent (session-resume session-close!)
  "(session-resume s), (session-close! s)
   Reopen a paused session and start its worker, and close a session and its
   streams (idempotent).")

(docdef :agent (channel? channel-post channel-fetch channel-ready? channel-count)
  "(channel? x), (channel-post ch value), (channel-fetch ch meta=false timeout=n),
   (channel-ready? ch), (channel-count ch)
   The bare-channel operations: test, send, receive, and inspect the reply
   queue. A channel's directions are from the owner's point of view -- out
   carries a message to the agent, in its replies.")

(docdef :agent (channel-hook! channel-unhook! channel-close!)
  "(channel-hook! ch form), (channel-unhook! ch form), (channel-close! ch)
   Install and remove an on-receive hook, and stop the channel.")

# ---- ssh -----------------------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :ssh ssh-server
  "(ssh-server [name] option …)
   Start an SSH server, a process resource named by a key, and return the key
   (generated when absent). Only air or an admin may start one, and it admits
   exactly the users its owner may assume; public-key auth is the only method. An
   interactive pty login opens a default (ui-shell …).")

(docdef :ssh ssh-servers
  "(ssh-servers)
   The running servers the caller may see, keyed by name.")

(docdef :ssh ssh-host-key
  "(ssh-host-key name private=false)
   The host's public key line (for known_hosts), or the private key when the
   caller is the owner or air.")

(docdef :ssh ssh-close!
  "(ssh-close! name)
   Stop listening and let the sessions drain.")

# ---- events and durable tasks ---------------------------------------------------

(docdef :event (do-at do-in tx-at tx-in)
  "(do-at time body …), (do-in interval body …), (tx-at …), (tx-in …)
   Schedule a durable event in the creator's $.config.events, run it in its own
   task, and return the promise it delivers. do-at fires at a time; do-in after
   an interval and is one-shot unless repeat=true. The tx- forms run the body as
   one transaction, so an escaping error aborts its writes. Options are name=,
   bindings=, and (for -in forms) repeat=.")

(docdef :event checkpoint
  "(checkpoint form)
   In a durable task run, return the value a previous attempt recorded, or
   evaluate form, record it, and return it; outside a durable run it just
   evaluates form. A checkpoint inside an open transaction raises.")

(docdef :event (events tasks)
  "(events [user]), (tasks [user])
   The caller's events or durable tasks as a mapping from name to summary.")

(docdef :event (event task-info)
  "(event x [user]), (task-info x [user])
   One summary, or (error :event-not-found …). x is a name or a promise.")

(docdef :event (cancel-event cancel-task)
  "(cancel-event x [user]), (cancel-task x [user])
   Cancel a job: an undelivered promise is settled with
   (error :event-cancelled …) and the status becomes :cancelled. Cancelling a
   terminal job is an idempotent no-op. A running body notices at its next
   cancellation point.")

(docdef :event (events-resume tasks-resume)
  "(events-resume [user]), (tasks-resume [user])
   Flip an interrupted :running record to :pending and start a worker for every
   non-terminal record; returns (resumed=n). air does this on startup.")

# ---- layouts and libraries -------------------------------------------------------

(docdef :layout layout
  "(layout pattern head=n indent=m map=inline|block)
   Declare how forms matching pattern break when printed. The declaration is
   structural (literals, wildcards, a final rest marker; no guards, no map
   entries), user declarations are tried newest-first ahead of the built-in
   defaults, head= pins the first n sequence elements to the opening line,
   indent= is a column offset or the symbol align, and map=block gives each
   key=value its own line. A process-global registration, not a definition.")

(docdef :layout layouts
  "(layouts)
   The registered user declarations, newest last, as a held list.")

(docdef :layout clear-layout
  "(clear-layout :head), (clear-layout :all)
   Drop user layout declarations; the built-in defaults cannot be removed.")

(docdef :layout reset-layouts
  "(reset-layouts)
   Drop every user layout declaration (the defaults stay).")

(docdef :library load-air
  "(load-air path …)
   Parse every file before evaluating any form, then evaluate all of them in one
   transaction; relative paths resolve against the loading file's directory. A
   library is a <name>/init.air directory found on AIR_LIBPATH, loaded lazily on
   first reference as .lib.<name>.")

# ---- iteration helpers, late additions to :control ----------------------------

(docdef :control nest
  "(nest f n x)
   Apply f exactly n times to x: (nest (?x -> (2 * x)) 5 1) is 32.")

(docdef :control nest-list
  "(nest-list f n x)
   Every value along the way, from x to the final one -- n + 1 elements.")

(docdef :control nest-while
  "(nest-while f pred x; list=false)
   Apply f while (pred value) holds, testing before each application, so a value
   that already fails pred is returned unchanged. list=true returns every value
   along the way instead of the last. A predicate that never fails is stopped by
   the reduction budget.")

# ---- control flow that sits with the error family -----------------------------

(docdef :error throw
  "(throw), (throw value), (throw tag value)
   Return early out of the innermost enclosing block whose catch= names the tag
   (a held literal, or ~tag for a computed one); the value is delivered as the
   block's value. With no tag the tag is true; with no value it is naught. An
   unmatched throw is the error (error :uncaught-throw tag value).")

(docdef :error notify
  "(notify data…), (notify a signal object)
   Dispatch a signal to the innermost matching (handle pattern …) clause, which
   is content-matched, innermost first, and return the handler's value. With one
   positional argument and no map entries an existing signal object is
   dispatched as itself; otherwise the evaluated arguments and map build the
   signal. Nothing matching raises (error :unhandled-signal signal).")

(docdef :error notify-or
  "(notify-or default data…)
   Like notify, but when no handler matches it holds default and evaluates it
   only then.")

(docdef :error try
  "(try …)
   A tombstone: try is no longer supported and raises. Use
   (do … (except …)).")
